# Email Sending

The Gmail connector wraps URLs in a `https://www.google.com/url?q=...` redirect
at send time. What the recipient sees depends on how the message is sent:

- Plain-text `body` only: the URL text itself is rewritten, and the generated
  HTML shows the ugly Google redirect as visible text. Never do this when the
  message contains a URL, domain, or code snippet with a URL.
- `body` plus `htmlBody`: the visible text stays exactly as written and only
  the invisible `href` is wrapped, which works fine for the reader.

Rules:

- Always pass `htmlBody` alongside `body` when sending or replying. Build the
  HTML from the same text: paragraphs as `<p>` or `<br/>`, URLs written as-is
  or as `<a href>` anchors, code snippets HTML-escaped inside `<code>`.
- Show the draft in chat and wait for "send" before sending.
- After sending anything with links, read the sent copy back once and confirm
  the visible text is clean.

